irc
Usage: irc [#channel] [nick] [server]

Reach irc over tls in one word.

No client, nothing new in the image: it brings up the tls tunnel and ii,
the two binaries that already ship, wired the one way that reaches a real
network.

With no argument it just prints the paths and returns -- a channel is a
directory you tail -f and an in fifo you echo to, so every text tool on
this system still works on the log.

	irc			bring the link up, print the paths
	irc #chan		open that channel as one screen
	irc #chan nick server	override nick and server

`irc #chan` is a live one screen: incoming scrolls the top rows, you
type at the bottom, both from the same two fifos, on a plain vt320 with
no client. A line starting with / is a server command; anything else is
a message. Ctrl-C or /quit leaves.

The arguments are read the way they look: a leading #channel, then a
nick, then a server, each optional. Nick defaults to xos, server to
irc.libera.chat, and the port is always 6697 -- tls only. The tunnel
checks the certificate against the anchors compiled into tlstunnel, not
a file on disk anyone could edit.

A registered nick's password rides the IRC_PASS environment variable,
read by name so it never lands in argv or the shell history. If the
tunnel never comes up the clock is unsynced or the certificate was
refused; the reason is in the tunnel's log, not lost to the console.

See also: ii, tlstunnel, tail
